CloudBurrow patch 86 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1662). Needs 0002, 0084 and 0085.

The emulator kept every query job's rows as JSON in its jobs table, for
jobs.getQueryResults, up to 256 MiB of JSON (patch 0002), even when the
job had written them to a destination table: CloudBurrow's front gives
each query job that names no destination a table of its own in its hidden
dataset (_cloudburrow_query_results), so every such result was held
twice. Measured after 5,000 jobs and 100 query jobs of the region's
INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS (about 5,100 rows each, arm64, Docker Desktop):
the temporary file (server.TempStorage) reached 605 MB, most of it the
JSON copies.

A jobs.insert that carries the header X-Cloudburrow-Result-In-Table, of
a query whose destination table the job makes, now keeps its result
without the rows: the schema, totalRows and the job's statistics are kept
as before, with a reference to the table. jobs.getQueryResults of it reads
the rows from the table (SELECT * in the table's order, as tabledata.list
reads them), passed through JSON as patch 0002 kept them, and pages them
as before; once the table is deleted, it answers 404 notFound "Not found:
Table project:dataset.table". A result that a SELECT * of the table would
not give back keeps its copy: one with a column named with a leading
underscore, as a pseudo-column is (_PARTITIONTIME, which a SELECT * leaves
out), and one with a FLOAT64 -0, which the engine writes to a table as 0.
Without the header nothing changes. The front sets the header on the jobs it gives a table, and
removes it from its clients' requests.

cloudburrow_result_in_table_test.go (TestResultReadFromItsTable) inserts
each of seven queries (250 rows; every scalar type, arrays, structs, a
range, NaN and infinities; no rows; a -0; columns named _PARTITIONTIME
and _PARTITIONDATE) twice, with and without the
header, and compares jobs.getQueryResults of both through eleven pagings
and formats, and jobs.get without its times; it then deletes the tables:
a job read from its table is 404, a job with its copy still answers. It
fails without the patch, and without either exception.

